                    Lightning Filelister v1.2 (for Zeus BBS)
                           copyright 1996 Rod Schnell



Description
~~~~~~~~~~~
 A fast, feature packed, filelist generator for Zeus BBS with many powerfull
 configuration options, capable of simultaneously creating up to 6 different
 output lists, in 3 different formats (Amiga txt, IBM txt and AmigaGuide).


Features
~~~~~~~~
  - Extremely fast and uses memory very efficiently, yet can be configured
    to run faster or use less memory if desired.
  - Files can be sorted in several different ways. By alphabet, size or date
    and in reverse or forward order.
  - Outputs up to 6 different optional file lists at the same time. There
    are 3 different filelist formats, Amiga text, IBM text and AmigaGuide,
    each of which can be output in both all and new files versions.
  - Areas to be included in the lists are configured with a simple numeric
    range string rather than by using lengthy lists of confusing keyword
    options like other filelisters. Lightning must be registered to enable
    this option, an unregistered Lightning will allways list all areas,
    regardless of this keyword.
  - Config file path is a command line option. Lightning can be run multiple
    times with completely different configuration options. This makes it
    possible to do things such as generate multiple output lists that are
    similar but use different sets of areas, larger or smaller new files
    lists and much more.
  - Files in AmigaGuide lists can optionally have FREQ buttons, which when
    clicked on will generate a Fidonet File REQuest to the system the
    filelist is from.
  - Supports optional insert files, both headers and footers, for each list
    and for each area within each list as well as a couple special insert
    files for the AmigaGuide lists.
  - Can use either truncated file descriptions that allow everything to fit
    on a single 80 character line, or full (long) file descriptions which
    are formated (line wrapped and indented) to fit.
  - Many other powerful configurations options.

  * See comments in the included example configuration file for details on
    all configuration options.


Installation
~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Simply copy the contents of the included Config directory to Zeus:Config
  and copy the main executeable, Lightning, anywhere you like.

  Then, edit the config file, Zeus:Config/Lightning/Lightning.cfg, to suit,
  paying particular attention to ensure output file paths are valid for your
  system. You may also wish to edit or delete the example Insert files or add
  new insert files of your own.


Useage
~~~~~~
  Next run Lightning... Lightning takes one optional argument, the full path
  to it's config file. If the config file path is not given, Lightning uses
  the default config file Zeus:Config/Lightning/Lightning.cfg.

  Depending on the number of files being listed, it may take a few seconds or
  several minutes to generate the output files. As a benchmark, using reverse
  date sort method with 256 file buffers, long descriptions, freq buttons, the
  included example insert files and a 30 day new files list, it took exactly
  3 minutes to list 7800+ files to all 6 output files on a standard,
  unaccelerated A4000/040.


Lightning.rexx
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  A frontend script, Lightning.rexx, is also included, which will run
  Lightning to generate the lists, then archive them and upload both the
  original unarchived lists and the archived lists to the BBS. These chores
  are done with a script because it's far more flexible than having Lightning
  do them itself.

  Before running this script, you will first have to edit the configuration
  variables (near the top of the script) to use the appropriate area number,
  filenames, path, etc., for your system.

  This script can be launched from another script, a shell or cron using the
  'rx' command.

  For example, to launch from a shell, AmigaDos script or cron:

             rx 'Lightning.rexx'

  To launch from another Arexx script:

             address command 'rx Lightning.rexx'

  Requirements: Lightning.rexx requires Zip and Lha be located somewhere in
                your path. It has been tested and is known to work with Zip
                v2.0.1 and lha v1.38.


AreaHeaders.rexx
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Arexx script to generate simple Header files for all file areas. Uses CRLF
  line termination for IBM headers too.

  When you run this script, it will generate header files (6 per area) which
  contain centered text with the area number, name and a 'Files checked using
  Virus_Checker...' note in t:. Then simply copy the headers you want to use
  to Lightning's Inserts directory and edit them to suit.
  
  Example usage: rx AreaHeaders.rexx


Notes on Enforcer hits
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  If you should happen to encounter any Enforcer hits, you likely have
  corrupt file indices and/or descriptions. To fix this (which will cause
  a lot of other problems too), you will need to do a full rebuild and
  renumber of your file areas.

  In addition, when running Enforcer, you should allways run SegTracker too.
  Without the extra info SegTracker provides, Enforcer reports are not very
  accurate and are virtually useless for debugging purposes.


History
~~~~~~~
  v1.0 betas
  
  - Several beta versions released to IRC #zeus participants for testing.

  v1.0 release

  - Wrote code to enable AREAS keyword.
  - Added file description comments to output files.
  - Changed default config file from Zeus:Config/File/Lightning.cfg to
    Zeus:Config/Lightning/Lightning.cfg. Suggested by AOBH.
  - Several minor (mostly cosmetic) changes.
  - Wrote frontend script Lightning.rexx.
  - No bugs were reported by the beta testers, so hopefully there aren't
    any.

  28-Apr-96

  - Rewrote Lightning.rexx, file archiver/upload script to not use
    Zuploader.

  v1.1 release
  
  - Added keyword NOREGNAME to prevent Lightning from printing the registered
    sysop's name in output files. Suggested by Jason Loxam.
  
  v1.2 (05-Jan-97)

  - Bugfix, with the registered version the AREAS keyword was not being used
    when -both- amigaguide files where turned off. Bug report by Joe Clayton.

